TeX Live collection set, built from the upstream TeX Live release in the
TeX Live SVN repository.

This is one pkgbase (`texlive-texmf`) that produces 23 packages:

  texlive-meta + 22 non-lang collections:
  basic bibtexextra binextra context fontsextra fontsrecommended fontutils
  formatsextra games humanities latex latexextra latexrecommended luatex
  mathscience metapost music pictures plaingeneric pstricks publishers xetex

`texlive-meta` is the entry point: it depends on exactly those 22
collections, so building this recipe installs the same set as
`paru -S texlive-meta`. The builder installs every split of a pkgbase, so all
23 packages are installed together.

There is no `build()`: texmf-dist is data. `prepare()` splits the checked-out
texmf tree into one directory per collection by reading
`tlpkg/texlive.tlpdb`, and each `package_texlive-<collection>()` moves its
directory into `$pkgdir`.

How the split loop works
-----------------------

The loop is the recipe's hot path and is deliberately batched — measured, not
assumed: rescanning the 18.7 MB tlpdb per package costs 4,115 full passes, and
one `mkdir -p` + one `mv` per file costs 301k process spawns for the 150k
runfiles. Instead:

  * `awk` cuts the tlpdb into one file per package section in a single pass
    (`.tlpdb/blocks/` under `$srcdir`, removed when `prepare()` ends);
  * runfiles, `execute AddFormat`/`addMap`/`AddHyphen` and the `bin/x86_64-linux`
    links are extracted from those sections with shell builtins, so a package
    costs no process at all;
  * renames are deferred and applied per collection, sorted by destination, so
    one `mv -t` moves every file of a directory (500 per call, in case a single
    directory — `fonts/tfm/google/noto` — holds thousands).

That is 0.94 s instead of 13.79 s for `fontsrecommended` (5,299 files, 14.7x),
and 37 instead of 2,482 process spawns on the synthetic fixture tree.

**The split moves files, it does not copy them.** `texmf-dist` is therefore
consumed: running `prepare()` a second time over the same tree would produce
packages with files silently missing. `prepare()` counts every runfiles entry
that is absent and refuses to continue (naming the count, five of the paths, and
the remedy — `rm -rf src`, which makes makepkg check out a fresh tree). A clean
checkout has zero absent entries; this checkout had 13,870 of 150,746.

`tests/texlive-split.sh` pins all of that: it runs the previous implementation
(frozen as `tests/assets/texlive-split-legacy.sh`) and the current one over the
same synthetic tree and requires identical tree listings (type, mode, path,
symlink target), identical content hashes, identical `pkgdesc-*`/`depends-*`/
`packages-*`/`.fmts`/`.maps`/`.dat*`, a large spawn reduction, and the refusal
on a depleted tree.

Disk cost is worth knowing before starting: the SVN working copies carry a
9.1 GB `.svn/pristine` shadow of every file, so `texmf-dist` occupies ~19 GB and
`src/texmf-dist` another ~19 GB.

Why the delivered set is trimmed
--------------------------------

Upstream also ships 17 `lang*` collections and `texlive-doc`. They are
deliberately dropped here (see the header comment in the PKGBUILD): `lang*`
splits and the documentation split are outside the maintained target, and
`texlive-meta`'s dependencies only cover the non-lang collections, so removing
them keeps every retained package content-identical to the Arch package of the
same release. Dropping whole splits is the only optimisation that applies to a
data package; there is no compiler, so no ISA, LTO, or PGO phase exists.

`texmf-dist/doc/` and `texmf-dist/source/` are part of the single `texmf-dist`
source, so they are still downloaded even though nothing packages them. That is
upstream's layout; only the packaging work and the installed size are saved.

Requirements
------------

  * `subversion` (makedepends) and a route to `svn://tug.org` on TCP 3690.
    That is the only endpoint: `https://svn.tug.org/texlive/` (and
    `http://tug.org/svn/texlive/`, which redirects to it) answer HTTP 406 to
    `svn ls`, so it is not a Subversion-over-HTTP server. If port 3690 is
    blocked there is no drop-in URL alternative for these three sources.
  * `texlive-bin` from the repositories (installed at 2026.0-2.1 here). It
    provides `/usr/bin/*`, which the pacman hooks installed by `texlive-basic`
    and `texlive-context` call (mktexlsr, fmtutil, updmap, mtxrun). It is not
    built by this workspace.
  * The checkout stays in the recipe directory (SRCDEST defaults to it) and is
    cached between builds. Expect ~38 GB on disk, not the ~3.5 GB of data: the
    three SVN working copies keep a 9.1 GB `.svn/pristine` shadow each, so
    `texmf-dist` costs ~19 GB and `src/texmf-dist` another ~19 GB.

Building
--------

  $ sudo fish build-all.fish --no-deps --install texlive-texmf

Or by hand, from this directory:

  $ makepkg -sr
  $ pacman -U texlive-*.pkg.tar.zst

After installing, the hooks regenerate the format and font maps. Verify with:

  $ kpsewhich latex.ltx
  $ texdoc --version

Keep the workspace packages protected from repository upgrades (golden rule 9).
The closure for this recipe is:

  $ comm -23 \
      <(bash -c 'source PKGBUILD && printf "%s\n" "${pkgbase}" "${pkgname[@]}"' | sort -u) \
      <(pacman-conf IgnorePkg | sort -u)

An empty result means every name is covered by `/etc/pacman.conf`.

Updating to a new upstream release
----------------------------------

TeX Live publishes point releases inside a year (`2024.0`, `2024.1`, ...,
`2026.1`), so this is a two-line bump:

  1. Newest tag. `.nvchecker.toml` ships the same signal upstream tracks
     (`regex = 'tags/texlive-([\d.]+)'` against
     https://git.texlive.info/texlive/); by hand:

       $ svn ls svn://tug.org/texlive/tags/ | sort -V | tail -1

  2. Set `pkgver` to that release, then pin `_rev` to the revision you want to
     build (Arch's value is the repository revision at packaging time) and
     regenerate metadata:

       $ svn info "svn://tug.org/texlive/tags/texlive-<version>/Master/tlpkg" |
           sed -n 's/^Revision: //p'
       $ makepkg --printsrcinfo > .SRCINFO

     `Revision:` is the repository revision, which is what the three
     `#revision=` pins mean. Do **not** use `Last Changed Rev`: it is only the
     revision at which that one directory last changed, and TeX Live keeps
     patching a tag after it is cut (for `texlive-2026.1`, `Last Changed Rev`
     for `tlpkg` is 78234 while the pinned 78408 additionally contains the
     changes from r78237 and r78408 under it).

  `_rev` is used as the `#revision=` pin on all three SVN sources, so the
  recipe always builds a fixed, reproducible tree; `svn checkout -r <rev>`
  fails loudly if the pinned revision predates the tag.

`list-collections` is the upstream helper that regenerates the `_collections`
array from `tlpkg/texlive.tlpdb`. It needs an installed TeX Live, and it
re-adds the `lang*` collections and `texlive-doc`: re-apply the trim from the
PKGBUILD header after running it.
